WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.661
(audience applauds)
- All righty then.

00:00:01.661 --> 00:00:03.800
Welcome, it is time for Drew's News.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.090
We cast out our line
(mimics reel whooshing)

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:10.400
and we reel in that good old news tuna.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.740
- Ah-ha?
- Catch a keeper.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.050
And with me at the desk
is my chicken of the sea,

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.300
Ross Matthews.
- You caught a big one.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:18.755
(audience applauds)
Hi, hi!

00:00:18.755 --> 00:00:21.440
(upbeat music)

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:25.170
- First up I found this
story to be a total delight.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.520
Our favorite girl, Dakota Johnson.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:28.353
That's right.

00:00:28.353 --> 00:00:31.600
Her and I do the Little Yellow Book

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.300
and her company, TeaTime,
we curate lists together.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.780
So she's a dear friend of our show

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:41.070
and she just revealed one
of her favorite hobbies.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:44.170
People reports that Dakota
says in her spare time

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.170
she loves to crash weddings.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.239
- [Ross] What?

00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:48.360
(audience gasps)
- How fun is that?

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.780
I just rewatched "Wedding
Crashers" this weekend too.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.290
So I was primed for this.

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:56.570
One time, she said she was holding up

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:58.930
a bride and groom in a chair.

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.150
I mean, what, Hava nagila? I love it.

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.941
And they looked down and they were like,

00:01:03.941 --> 00:01:06.184
"Oh, whoa, what are you doing here?

00:01:06.184 --> 00:01:07.017
(audience laughs)

00:01:07.017 --> 00:01:10.440
Dakota? You little minx,
I love this about you.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.840
All your layers.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.065
Come crash our show or
our wedding anytime soon.

00:01:15.065 --> 00:01:17.805
(audience applauds)

00:01:17.805 --> 00:01:19.860
Have you ever crashed anything, Rossy?

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.860
- I would never crash
a wedding in my life.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.570
Especially since I'm planning one now.

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:25.580
All I'm thinking to myself is
like, "That's three shrimp.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:26.740
That's three shrimp!"

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.440
Right? We plan ahead.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.340
- This is what's happening on TikTok.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.970
It's exactly what you think it is.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:36.970
Apparently you boil potato
chips out of the bag

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.890
and then mash 'em into a paste.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:44.020
And then you add in milk, butter or cream

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.030
and salt and pepper to taste.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:48.817
I think it sounds like
a goblin moment, right?

00:01:48.817 --> 00:01:49.690
- Oh definitely.
- Yeah.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:50.690
- Okay. So...

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:53.110
Sorry, I'm salivating.
- Uh-huh.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:56.030
- So this is, remember like, Idaho Spuds

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:57.550
and Potato Buds?

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:58.620
- Oh my God, the boxed potato.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.900
I didn't know mashed potatoes
came from an actual potato

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:01.733
until I was like 20.

00:02:01.733 --> 00:02:03.490
I thought it just was out of the box.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.530
- [Drew] So this, it feels like kind of a,

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:07.350
I don't know some version of that.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:08.320
- Yeah.
- Is that it?

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.140
- I want it to be really good, right?

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.140
- Well, one, two, three.
- Okay.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.600
- Okay. Ready?

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:14.433
- Thank you.

00:02:16.817 --> 00:02:18.079
- Hm.
- Yeah.

00:02:18.079 --> 00:02:19.390
- Y'all don't like it.
(audience laughs)

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:20.564
- No, we love it.

00:02:20.564 --> 00:02:21.397
- Oh. (laughs)

00:02:21.397 --> 00:02:23.010
- We get mad when we like it,

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:25.960
because then it's like, "Oh
no, now it's gonna be a thing".

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:26.793
- Okay.
- Yeah.

00:02:26.793 --> 00:02:28.594
- Oh, this sucks.
(audience laughs)

00:02:28.594 --> 00:02:31.950
- Now this is gonna haunt my dream.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.290
- Do you all practice
the five second rule?

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.793
Raise of hands, who does
the five second rule?

00:02:36.793 --> 00:02:37.695
- [Ross] Be honest, yeah.

00:02:37.695 --> 00:02:39.160
- [Drew] I mean, I've done
like the 10 second rule.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:39.993
- [Ross] Right?

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:42.020
- Okay, marthastewart.com reports

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.070
that scientists have actually tested

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:47.260
the five second rule in a lab.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.150
They dropped a piece of baloney.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:51.220
Well, that's just like...
- That's gross already.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:52.897
- That's like tape.
- Yeah, right?

00:02:52.897 --> 00:02:54.581
- Of course it's gonna pick something up.

00:02:54.581 --> 00:02:55.620
- (laughs) It's totally tape!

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.030
- And a piece of dry
bread on a surface with,

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:02.120
ugh, salmonella on it.
- Hmm?

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:05.110
- And they let it sit for
five seconds, 30 seconds

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:08.290
and then a full minute
and the results are in.

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:10.310
Regardless of how long it's sat there,

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:13.682
the food was very definitely contaminated.

00:03:13.682 --> 00:03:16.270
- [Ross] Oh man. (groans)

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:17.970
- Even after just five seconds

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.080
that baloney was salmonella city.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:21.350
- [Ross] I know.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:23.500
- [Both] Oh God.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.610
- Well, I mean, oh man.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.490
- I feel like there are two types

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:28.990
of five second rules in the world.

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.010
There's like five second
rule around people.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.800
And you're like, "No,
I'm not gonna, mm-mm."

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.430
And then there's the
five second rule at home,

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:36.920
where you're like that's
been there for like,

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:38.050
I don't know, a few minutes.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.997
I actually, one time
just found a jellybean

00:03:39.997 --> 00:03:41.750
(audience laughs)

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:42.940
And I ate it.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.300
- This story really spoke to me.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.460
The Baltimore Museum of Art
just opened a new exhibit

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:52.030
curated by their own security guards.

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:53.870
The Week, my favorite magazine,

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:56.550
reports the exhibit is
called "Guarding the Art"

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.200
and it's up through July.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:01.500
So the museum asked 17
of their security guards

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.610
that obviously, are in the,

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:06.080
you know, building all day, every day,

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.580
to select works from the collection

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.810
that were important and
meaningful to the security guards.

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:16.090
Guard Ben Bjork picked a
chair made out of pencils

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:18.500
by artist Jeremy Aldean,

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:21.260
because Ben says he spends
a lot lot of the day

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:23.609
thinking about sitting down.

00:04:23.609 --> 00:04:24.880
(audience laughs)
- Well yeah, right?

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:27.090
- Um, Ross?

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:30.250
- The fact that they got
to choose to curate a show

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:31.210
based on what they love,

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:34.470
just made me feel like it was
so accessible and so doable.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.700
I felt no, that's a show I wanna go see.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:38.629
So well done to this museum.

00:04:38.629 --> 00:04:41.212
(bright music)

